$$
\begin{aligned}
(-\frac{1}{2}) \cdot (-\frac{10}{3}) &= \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{10}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Now, we multiply the fractions by multiplying the numerators and denominators separately:

$$
\begin{array}{cc}\frac{1}{2} \cdot  \frac{10}{3} &  = \frac{1 \cdot  10}{2 \cdot  3} \\  &  = \frac{10}{2 \cdot  3} \\  &  = \frac{5 \cdot  2}{2 \cdot  3} \\  &  = \frac{5 \cdot  \cancel{2}}{\cancel{2} \cdot  3} \\  &  = \frac{5}{3}\end{array}
$$

